- op: discuss  # 7011
  at: '2026-09-06T19:17:15Z'  # 7011
  by: agent  # 7011
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7011
  clock: 4  # 7011
  nonce: '7011'  # 7011
  message: "OWNER RULING, 2026-09-06 (walk-through of owner-decision items, item 1 of 5).\nVerbatim: \"1.open 2. as you recommend 3. yes. re 'holding' delete the\nduplication. also, when a core name gets refused, the error message should\nexplicitly say 'use `<correct flag>` instead'. and for the 'unknown key\npriorty' example, that is relevant for a human typist, but for an LLM agent,\nthe more common sad path is something like 'item-priority' -- no misspelling,\njust a synonym. so the 'whoa' layer can refuse but needs to provide the\nlikely correction as well as the option to override with a --force flag --\nwhich is only allowed in the similar-but-nonidentical scenario. and that is\nsimilar-but-nonidentical after normalization.\"\n\nDECISIONS, as I read them (correct me on the item if wrong):\n  1. The custom dict stays OPEN. Option (a) reject-all-undeclared is\n     REJECTED. A genuinely novel key lands.\n  2. Declarations / cleanup: declare dogfood_anon_id on work_item; retire\n     `severity` (17 invariants) in favour of the core priority; fold\n     statement / root_cause / rationale on work items into description.\n     PRIORITY CHANGES GO THROUGH THE OWNER: where an item's severity value\n     disagrees with its existing priority, the disagreement is LISTED for\n     the owner, not resolved by the migration.\n  3. `scripts/tracker validate --strict` goes ON in the three CI callers\n     once the corpus is clean.\n  4. The stale .agent/tracker-workspace/config.yaml (still allows\n     `holding`, lacks deprecated_statuses and dogfood_anon_id) is a second\n     home: DELETE the duplication, one config.\n  5. Gate semantics, THREE TIERS keyed on NORMALIZATION (lowercase, strip\n     non-alphanumerics, singularize):\n       (i)  IDENTICAL to a core name after normalization (status, Status,\n            is_before, isBefore, Priority, tag/tags, pr-ref ...):\n            HARD REFUSAL, no override. Message MUST say\n            \"use `--<flag>` instead\", naming the dedicated flag.\n       (ii) SIMILAR BUT NOT IDENTICAL after normalization: REFUSED with the\n            likely correction, overridable with --force. --force is\n            accepted ONLY in this tier. The LLM-agent sad path is the\n            synonym / compound, not the typo: `item-priority`,\n            `item_status`, `severity`, `desc`, `summary`, `rank`, `state`.\n            Detectors, all stdlib: token containment on the normalized key\n            (item_priority -> {item, priority}), curated alias table\n            (severity->priority, state->status, desc/summary->description,\n            rank->priority), edit distance <=2 on the normalized string,\n            and VALUE SHAPE (value is a status name / P0-P4 / a comma-list\n            of catalogue tags / proquint IDs / a PR number or SHA) which\n            names the intended flag even under a novel key.\n       (iii) NOT SIMILAR: lands (open dict). The corpus census line (\"no\n            work_item has ever carried 'rationale'; keys in use on this\n            kind: ...\") and the consequence sentence stay as a WARNING\n            here, create-anyway, same shape as the SimHash warning.\n     Edit distance never gates on its own: `fix` (d=2 from `id`) is a\n     declared key and must stay usable without --force -- a DECLARED\n     schema key is exempt from tier (ii) by construction.\n  6. Both parsers (--field, --add-field; add and update) share ONE\n     constant and ONE predicate with validation.py, so the gate and the\n     validator cannot drift.\n\nPOSITIVE CONTROLS for closure, behavioural: `--field Status=done` on a\nthrowaway item exits non-zero and prints \"use `--status` instead\";\n`--field item-priority=P2` exits non-zero, names --priority, and succeeds\nwith --force; `--field Status=done --force` is STILL refused; `--field\ncompletely_novel=1` lands with a warning; the census re-run reads 0\ncore-name shadows and 0 of the five retired keys on N items, N stated;\nCI runs validate --strict.\n\nNot started. Sequenced after the arc unless the owner says otherwise."  # 7011
